**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["7B.1"]}
E0001 is the office-attribution anchor only: Modi was PM throughout 2022, which lets the 2022 evidence be tied to his ruler-period.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Assets and Liabilities of the Union Council of Ministers (2021-2022) as on 31.03.2022","publisher":"Prime Minister of India","publication_date":"2022-08","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Shri-Narendra-Modi.pdf","claim":"Modi's 31 March 2022 asset filing listed Rs 22,382,504 in gross movable assets, no investments in bonds, debentures, shares or units, no immovable property, and a note that the only jointly held property had been donated.","locator":"PDF pp. 0-3, lines 15-19, 52-56, 129-132, 211-280","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO self-disclosure in a contemporaneous asset filing; strong for what Modi declared, though self-reported disclosures do not independently exclude undeclared interests.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"As of 31.03.2022, squarely within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct self-disclosure attributable to Modi as PM; useful for personal-separation and no-extraction questions, but not a full audit of hidden interests.","contrary_evidence":["The filing is self-reported and cannot rule out undeclared assets or benefits."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.9","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IFS Vivek Kumar appointed as PS to PM Modi","publisher":"The Times of India","publication_date":"2022-05-21","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/ifs-vivek-kumar-appointed-as-ps-to-pm-modi/articleshow/91712881.cms","claim":"The government appointed Vivek Kumar, an IFS officer of the 2004 batch, as Private Secretary to the Prime Minister at joint-secretary level; the article says he had been in the PMO since 2014.","locator":"story lines 122-139","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary reporting, but it quotes a DoPT release and gives a specific named appointment with clear period fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in May 2022 and directly describes a PMO staffing choice during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant: it is a staffing choice in Modi's immediate office and a sign of reliance on a career officer rather than a family or business associate.","contrary_evidence":["One professional appointment does not establish the overall staffing pattern of the PMO or government."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No evidence, Govt didn’t cooperate: SC panel on Pegasus","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/supreme-court-pegasus-spyware-case-8110566/","claim":"The Supreme Court-appointed Pegasus committee told the Court that it found no conclusive evidence in the phones examined but that the Central Government had not cooperated with the probe.","locator":"lines 45-60, 72-77","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The article reports the Chief Justice's summary of the panel's findings from open court, making it a strong proxy for the judicial record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The hearing and reported findings occurred in August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Central Government under Modi as a matter of state non-cooperation with an independent probe; it does not by itself prove Modi personally ordered concealment or spyware use.","contrary_evidence":["The panel also said the evidence it examined was inconclusive on Pegasus use in the sampled phones."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sale of 'less transparent', physical electoral bonds jumps before polls, show RTI replies","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-11-10","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/sale-of-less-transparent-physical-electoral-bonds-jumps-before-polls-show-rti-replies-8259628/","claim":"RTI replies showed that the government and RBI had debated electoral-bond design, with the RBI favoring digital-only bonds to reduce misuse while the Finance Ministry emphasized physical bonds to protect donor identity; physical sales rose before the 2022 Gujarat and Himachal Pradesh polls.","locator":"lines 110-130, especially 115-123 and 129-130","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Based on RTI-sourced official correspondence and a contemporaneous explanation of the scheme's design choices.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to the election cycle that year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material: the design choice belongs to Modi's government and benefited the ruling party's political-finance environment, though it is not personal enrichment evidence.","contrary_evidence":["The government argued the scheme still used KYC and banking channels and was meant to preserve donor anonymity from political victimization."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.8","7B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"On Electoral Bonds, RTI Body's Order After Election Panel's 'No Info' Reply","publisher":"NDTV / PTI","publication_date":"2022-06-22","url":"https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/on-electoral-bonds-transparency-watchdogs-unusual-order-for-poll-body-3089529","claim":"The Central Information Commission directed the Election Commission to swear that it had already supplied all information available with it on electoral bonds and that no further disclosable records existed in its files.","locator":"lines 290-300","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"A PTI-syndicated report of a CIC order; useful as a contemporaneous transparency record but weaker than the underlying order text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"June 2022, directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect to Modi: it concerns the opacity of the electoral-bond regime run under his government, not a personal act of enrichment or concealment.","contrary_evidence":["The Election Commission said it had already furnished all available records sought under the RTI request."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inside Modi's crackdown on the foreign press in India","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2024-11-13","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/news/inside-modis-crackdown-foreign-press-india","claim":"The article reports that between January 2020 and February 2022 foreign correspondents in India described summons by officials, review of files and spreadsheets on negative coverage, visa and permit uncertainty, and growing self-censorship and fear.","locator":"lines 217-230","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Retrospective but well-sourced synthesis from reported surveys, anonymous interviews, and press-freedom reporting; useful for pattern evidence, not a single event record.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The cited surveys cover January 2020 through February 2022, overlapping the target period, though the article itself is later.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant: it describes the Modi government's environment for scrutiny and foreign correspondents, with attribution limited by anonymized accounts and retrospective synthesis.","contrary_evidence":["The piece notes that some restrictions and summonses predated Modi's tenure and that prior governments also expressed displeasure with critical coverage."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I screened roughly 30 documents across PMO/PIB, the Supreme Court and related reporting, TOI, Indian Express, NDTV/PTI, Reuters Institute, and a handful of fact-check and allegation items.
I retained 6 distinct URLs because they gave the best ruler-specific signal on 2022 integrity questions.
I rejected items that were duplicates of the same Pegasus or electoral-bond episode, out-of-period follow-ups that did not add a 2022 act, allegation-only lawsuits and commentary, fact-checks showing other people misused Modi clips, and general corruption or censorship pieces that lacked a direct personal nexus to Modi's honesty, self-dealing, concealment, or corrective behavior.
I also kept 3B/4B-style repression material out unless it showed a direct integrity nexus such as obstruction of a probe, secrecy-friendly political finance design, or pressure on scrutiny.

**Remaining Gaps**
- I did not find direct 2022 evidence of Modi personally telling a provable falsehood and then correcting it.
- I did not find 2022 evidence of personal or family enrichment, hidden conflicts of interest, or quid-pro-quo procurement/licensing involving Modi himself.
- The strongest negative evidence is about government opacity and noncooperation with scrutiny, not direct personal self-dealing.
- More search could still help on archived campaign-finance records, affidavit comparisons, court filings, and PMO staffing history, but the current record already supports a mixed dossier with a strong transparency/obstruction theme and a limited positive disclosure theme.
